PUBLIC RESPONSE: H. None of these choices – the bedside description is that of congenital pulmonic stenosis, which is not listed.

A harsh systolic ejection murmur maximal at the left 2nd-3rd intercostal space / left upper sternal border, radiating to the left clavicle / neck, that is inspiratory accentuated and is accompanied by a palpable thrill in the pulmonary area is the classic auscultatory signature of right ventricular outflow obstruction. Inspiration augments right sided venous return and therefore accentuates a right sided ejection murmur; the thrill reflects a high velocity jet. Chronic pressure overload of the right ventricle gives right ventricular hypertrophy on ECG, exertional dyspnea, fatigue / light-headedness in adult life and, when the obstruction is significant in childhood, faint central cyanosis on exertion.

None of the listed lesions reproduces that obstructive picture:

* Ebstein anomaly → holosystolic tricuspid regurgitation murmur at the left lower sternal border with atrialized RV;
* Patent ductus arteriosus → continuous machinery murmur at the left upper sternal border;
* Mitral valve prolapse → midsystolic click ± late systolic murmur at the apex;
* Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with obstruction → dynamic LVOT systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border / apex, increases with Valsalva and decreases with inspiration;
* Tricuspid stenosis → diastolic rumbling at the left lower sternal border;
* Ventricular septal defect → harsh holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border;
* Atrial septal defect can give a pulmonary flow systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border that is inspiratory accentuated and is associated with RVH and, in the ostium primum / AV canal form, left axis deviation. That is the only listed lesion that can mimic an increased flow across the pulmonary valve and it is the usual board exam mimic for this auscultatory pattern. A pure ASD flow murmur, however, is typically soft without a palpable thrill and ASD is acyanotic; the history of faint childhood cyanosis, the loud ejection quality with a thrill and the degree of RV pressure overload described are far more typical of true pulmonic stenosis.

Strictly anatomically the murmur described is valvular pulmonary stenosis and that lesion is not offered. If a choice must be forced from the list the increased pulmonary flow murmur of an atrial septal defect, especially ostium primum with left axis deviation + RVH, is the closest physiologic mimic.

